From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
Date: Wed, 29 Jul 2026 20:06:28 +0200
Subject: media: gc2145: Allow the flicker avoidance to be turned off

Avoiding flicker is not free. The row period is stretched to a whole
number of mains periods, and the exposure the AEC picks is quantised to
the mains half period - 10 ms at 50 Hz, which in a 33 ms frame leaves it
three exposures to choose between and forces everything finer onto the
gain. Under daylight or LED there is no flicker to avoid and both of
those are pure loss, so stop masking the DISABLED menu entry out.

A zero mains frequency then means the row period is left at its
shortest, which is also the fastest the mode reads out, and the AEC gets
a continuous exposure range. Guard the row period search against it, as
it divides by the frequency.

Signed-off-by: Ondrej Jirman <megi@xff.cz>
---
 drivers/media/i2c/gc2145.c | 43 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 34 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/media/i2c/gc2145.c b/drivers/media/i2c/gc2145.c
index 111111111111..222222222222 100644
--- a/drivers/media/i2c/gc2145.c
+++ b/drivers/media/i2c/gc2145.c
@@ -1189,6 +1189,15 @@ static void gc2145_params_fit_hb(struct gc2145_sensor_params *p,
 {
 	unsigned long rt, power_line_ratio;
 
+	/*
+	 * Nothing to fit the row period to, so leave it at its shortest -
+	 * which is also the fastest the mode can be read out.
+	 */
+	if (!power_line_freq) {
+		p->hb = GC2145_HBLANK_REG_MIN;
+		return;
+	}
+
 	for (p->hb = GC2145_HBLANK_REG_MIN; p->hb < 4095; p->hb++) {
 		rt = gc2145_params_row_period(p);
 
@@ -1460,12 +1469,22 @@ static int gc2145_s_ctrl(struct v4l2_ctrl *ctrl)
 	switch (ctrl->id) {
 	case V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY:
 		/*
-		 * The row period is fitted to a whole number of mains
-		 * periods when the mode is programmed, so this only takes
-		 * effect the next time that happens.
+		 * Zero means there is no flicker to avoid, which leaves the
+		 * row period free to be as short as it can and the exposure
+		 * free to take any value. The row period is fitted when the
+		 * mode is programmed, so this takes full effect then.
 		 */
-		sensor->power_line_freq =
-			val == V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY_60HZ ? 60 : 50;
+		switch (val) {
+		case V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY_DISABLED:
+			sensor->power_line_freq = 0;
+			break;
+		case V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY_60HZ:
+			sensor->power_line_freq = 60;
+			break;
+		default:
+			sensor->power_line_freq = 50;
+			break;
+		}
 		sensor->pending_mode_change = true;
 		return 0;
 
@@ -1745,14 +1764,20 @@ static int gc2145_init_controls(struct gc2145_dev *sensor)
 				       ae_bias_menu_values);
 
 	/*
-	 * Flicker avoidance is done by fitting the row period to the mains
-	 * period, so only the two mains frequencies are selectable - there
-	 * is nothing to disable and no detector to run.
+	 * Flicker avoidance costs something, so it can be turned off: the
+	 * row period is stretched to a whole number of mains periods, and
+	 * the exposure the AEC picks is quantised to the mains half period.
+	 * Under daylight or LED there is no flicker to avoid and both are
+	 * pure loss - the quantum is 10 ms at 50 Hz, which in a 33 ms frame
+	 * leaves the AEC three exposures to choose between.
+	 *
+	 * There is no detector, so this says what the light is rather than
+	 * asking for it to be measured.
 	 */
 	ctrls->pl_freq = v4l2_ctrl_new_std_menu(hdl, ops,
 						V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY,
 						V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY_60HZ,
-						BIT(V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY_DISABLED),
+						0,
 						V4L2_CID_POWER_LINE_FREQUENCY_50HZ);
 
 	/*
